// F310: Titius-Bode regularity in exoplanet systems is spacing, not sequence
// The BuildTeX source of this whitepaper. Built with: buildc run, then python -m buildtex.
mod doc;
use doc::*;

fn main() ~ Console + FileSystem {
    let mut d = doc_new("Titius-Bode regularity in exoplanet systems is spacing, not sequence");
    theme(&mut d, "paper");
    meta(&mut d, "pdf_author", "Zain Dana Harper");
    meta(&mut d, "subject", "A preregistered test of the Titius-Bode rule on 122 exoplanet systems against order-shuffled and pooled-gap controls");
    meta(&mut d, "footer", "F310, Titius-Bode regularity is spacing, version 1.0");
    blk(&mut d, "title", "Titius-Bode regularity in exoplanet systems is spacing, not sequence");
    blk(&mut d, "subtitle", "A preregistered test on 122 systems from the NASA Exoplanet Archive, with order-shuffle and pooled-gap controls");
    blk(&mut d, "author", "Zain Dana Harper");
    blk(&mut d, "meta", "Version 1.0, 9 November 2026. Finding F310 in the findings ledger. Signal (tier B).");
    blk(&mut d, "abstracthead", "Abstract");
    blk(&mut d, "abstract", "The Titius-Bode rule says planets sit at distances that grow by a steady ratio, and its generalised forms have been used to predict planets nobody has seen. We asked whether exoplanet spacing follows such a rule beyond what each system's own set of gaps explains. On 122 systems with four or more planets (543 planets) from the NASA Exoplanet Archive, three forms of the rule were scored against two controls fixed in advance. The real systems beat a control that draws gaps from the whole catalogue (p 0.0005, 2,000 draws), and none of the three forms beat a control that keeps each system's gaps and shuffles their order (p 0.85, 0.995 and 0.68). When an interior planet is dropped, the geometric mean of its two neighbours predicts its period better than the rule does: median error 0.107 against 0.131 in log period, a ratio of 1.23. The regularity is real, and it is the regularity of similar gaps inside one system. The order of those gaps adds nothing we could detect. The preregistered law-like verdict failed.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:reader", "What this means for a reader");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "If a paper says a Titius-Bode fit predicts a missing planet at a given period, ask what the two neighbouring planets alone would predict. A Titius-Bode rule, in planetary astronomy, is any formula that places a system's planets at distances or periods growing by a near-constant ratio from one to the next; it is a description of where planets sit and carries no model of how they formed or migrated. In our sample the neighbours win. A system's planets do tend to sit at similar spacing ratios, a pattern known from Kepler data as \"peas in a pod\" {cite:weiss2018}, and any rule that fits a geometric sequence will fit such a system well. That good fit is not evidence that the sequence itself is a law. The exoplanet survey phrase covers planets inside one system being alike in size and in spacing ratio; it states no rule for the order of the gaps.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:intro", "Introduction");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Johann Titius noticed in 1766 that the planets then known sit near distances given by a simple doubling sequence, and Johann Bode popularised it. The rule matched Uranus and the asteroid belt and missed Neptune. It has been rewritten many times since. Blagg {cite:blagg1913} fitted the Solar System and the moons of the giant planets with a periodic correction to a geometric progression; Richardson {cite:richardson1945} gave another version.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The question of whether the rule means anything was put sharply by Hayes and Tremaine {cite:hayes1998}. They drew random planetary systems, rejected the ones that would be unstable, and found that the survivors fit a Titius-Bode law about as well as the Solar System. Their conclusion was that stable systems tend to be regularly spaced, and that this is the whole content of the rule.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Exoplanets gave the rule new material. Bovaird and Lineweaver {cite:bovaird2013} fitted a generalised Titius-Bode relation to multi-planet systems, allowed for unseen planets in the gaps, and predicted 141 new planets. Huang and Bakos {cite:huang2014} searched the Kepler data for 97 of those predictions and found 5. Bovaird, Lineweaver and Jacobsen {cite:bovaird2015} replied that many predicted planets would not transit and should not be expected in the data, and ranked new predictions by their chance of transiting.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "This paper adds one control to that argument. A control, in the sense of experimental design, is a copy of the data with one structure removed and scored exactly like the real data; a comparison with another catalogue or a published number would not count as one. A rule about a sequence should beat a version of the same system with its gaps put in a different order. If it does not, the rule describes the sizes of the gaps and nothing about their arrangement.");
    attributed(&mut d, "figure", "fig:plate", "Robert P. Traxler, Fig. IV, \"Sectional side view of the Sun with Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune etc.\", from The Principles of Mechanics as Applied to the Solar System (San Francisco, 1889) {cite:traxler1889}. Traxler drew his plates to argue for a repelling force from the Sun, his own theory in 1889, which physics did not adopt. The orbit rings sit at the spacings a Titius-Bode rule tries to describe. Public domain; scan from the Internet Archive, University of California Libraries.", "src=plate-traxler-fig4.png;width=0.5;alt=A white-on-black 1889 engraving of the Sun at the centre of concentric orbit rings with the outer planets and their moons");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:prereg", "Preregistration and design");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The design was written and hash-locked on 31 October 2026 as a preregistration, after the archive file was fetched and its first two rows were seen and before any spacing was computed. The practice comes from clinical trials and psychology: a dated plan fixed before any result is seen, so a plan revised after a look at the outcome does not qualify. The decision it informs was stated in the same file: if the rule fails, it is reported as spacing, and predicted-planet claims built on it get no weight in our work.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The locked file names the data, the three forms, the per-system statistic, the leave-one-out prediction test, the planted systems, both controls, the forecasts and the kill conditions. Leave-one-out is a validation method from statistics: drop one planet, fit the rest and predict the one dropped, so no planet is scored by a fit that has seen it. The planted systems are our planted check, synthetic systems with a known regularity run through the same analysis first to show the score can find regularity when it is there, which no check on real data can show. A kill condition is a rule in the plan that decides against the claim when it is met, with no room for judgement after the run. Nothing was added after the run. The preregistration's SHA-256 is given in the reproduction section. SHA-256 is a cryptographic hash, a 64-character fingerprint of a file that changes if any byte changes; it shows the plan was not edited and keeps nothing secret, since it is not encryption.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The two controls are our own constructions, set out in the methods. The order-shuffle control keeps each system's own gaps and puts them in a random order. The pooled-gap control rebuilds each system from gaps drawn from the whole sample. Neither brings in any planet from outside the 122 systems.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Kill conditions, without discretion:");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Void if the planted systems score at or below the pooled-gap control's 95th percentile, or if fewer than 60 systems qualify. A void run, in our usage, says nothing for or against the rule, and differs from a null result, which comes from an instrument that works.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Law-like only if the plain form beats the order-shuffle control's 95th percentile and the leave-one-out ratio is below 1. Otherwise the rule is reported as explained by spacing. Law-like is our label for a rule whose sequence carries information past the sizes of the gaps; regularity that similar gaps alone produce falls outside it.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:data", "Data and provenance");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The data are the NASA Exoplanet Archive Planetary Systems Composite Parameters table {cite:nasa}, retrieved on 31 October 2026 through the archive's TAP service. TAP, the Table Access Protocol set by the International Virtual Observatory Alliance, is the standard query interface of astronomical archives; it fetches rows and applies no selection of its own. A system qualified with at least four listed planets that have orbital periods and a controversy flag of zero. The archive sets that flag to 1 when a planet's existence has been questioned in the published literature, so 0 means no published dispute, which is weaker than independent confirmation. This gave 122 systems and 543 planets. The archive is public and places no restriction on use.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Two selection effects shape the sample. Transit surveys find short periods and planets whose orbits are close to edge-on, so long periods and tilted planets are missing, and the gaps we see are partly survey-shaped. A transit survey, in observational astronomy, finds a planet by the dip in starlight as it crosses its star's disc; planets found only by other methods, such as radial velocity, are outside that term. Multi-planet entries in the catalogue are also a selected set. The order-shuffle control keeps both effects, because it uses the same gaps from the same systems. That is why it is the fair control for a claim about order.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:methods", "Methods");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "The three forms");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "For a system with planets sorted by period, let {m:P_n} be the period of the {m:n}th planet. The plain form is log-linear in the index:");
    labelled(&mut d, "eq", "eq:plain", "\\ln P_n = a + b n");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The Blagg-Richardson style form adds an offset period {m:P_0}, a three-parameter form in period:");
    labelled(&mut d, "eq", "eq:blagg", "\\ln (P_n - P_0) = a + b n");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The gapped form, in the style of Bovaird and Lineweaver, replaces the index by integers {m:k_n} that may skip up to two places, so that up to two unseen planets can be inserted wherever they lower the residual:");
    labelled(&mut d, "eq", "eq:gapped", "\\ln P_n = a + b k_n");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "All three forms were written from the published descriptions as remembered, not copied from the papers. Blagg's own formula has a periodic correction term that eq. {ref:eq:blagg} does not carry. The result is therefore about these three versions.");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "The regularity score");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "For system {m:s}, let {m:\\sigma_s} be the root mean square residual of the fit in {m:\\ln P}, and {m:g_s} the mean gap in {m:\\ln P} between neighbouring planets. The per-system statistic divides one by the other, so that systems with wide and narrow spacing are compared on one scale. The residual term is from statistics: the square root of the mean squared difference between fitted and observed {m:\\ln P}, a measure of scatter about the fit that leaves out any offset the fit has already absorbed:");
    labelled(&mut d, "eq", "eq:r", "r_s = \\frac{\\sigma_s}{g_s}");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The sample score {m:S} is minus the median of {m:r_s} over the systems, so a higher score means more regular. Each form has its own score.");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "Prediction by leave-one-out");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "For each interior planet {m:j}, the planet is dropped, the plain form is fitted to the rest, and the fitted {m:\\ln P_j} is compared with the true value. The baseline predicts {m:\\ln P_j} as the mean of its two neighbours' {m:\\ln P}, which is the geometric mean of their periods. The geometric mean of two numbers is the square root of their product, the plain average taken on a log scale; it is smaller than the ordinary average whenever the two differ. The ratio of median absolute errors is");
    labelled(&mut d, "eq", "eq:loo", "L = \\frac{M_{fit} }{M_{nb} }");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "where {m:M_{fit} } is the median error of the fit and {m:M_{nb} } that of the neighbour baseline. A ratio above 1 means the neighbours win.");
    blk(&mut d, "subsection", "Controls");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The order-shuffle control keeps each system's set of gaps in {m:\\ln P} and puts them in a random order, then rebuilds the periods from the innermost planet. The pooled-gap control rebuilds each system from gaps drawn at random from all systems' gaps. Each control is scored with the same form and the same freedom as the real data; for the gapped form that includes the inserted planets. The plain form's controls ran 2,000 draws each and the other two forms' order controls ran 200 each.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The p value reported for each comparison is the share of control draws scoring at or above the real score, counted with one added to both the numerator and the denominator. It is a p value from draws, as in permutation statistics, and assumes no textbook distribution:");
    labelled(&mut d, "eq", "eq:p", "p = \\frac{k + 1}{n + 1}");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "where {m:k} is the number of draws at or above the real score and {m:n} the number of draws. This is why the smallest possible value at 2,000 draws is 0.0005.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:controls", "Controls and planted checks");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The instrument was checked on planted systems before the real data were read. Planted systems have exactly log-linear periods with 5 percent jitter and the same numbers of planets as the real systems. They scored -0.050 against the pooled-gap control's 95th percentile of -0.190, so the score sees regularity when it is there. The void condition did not fire.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The two controls ask different questions, and they are read in that order. Beating the pooled-gap control says the gaps inside a system resemble each other more than gaps drawn from other systems. Beating the order-shuffle control would say the order of those gaps is regular as well. Only the second is a claim about a sequence.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:results", "Results");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Table {ref:tab:scores} gives the score of each form against its controls, and Figure {ref:fig:reg} draws the same numbers. The plain form scores -0.157. The real systems are far more regular than systems built from pooled gaps. They are not more regular than their own gaps in a shuffled order: the real score sits inside the order-shuffle range for all three forms.");
    labelled(&mut d, "tablecap", "tab:scores", "Regularity scores against the two controls. The interval is the 5th to 95th percentile of the control draws.");
    attributed(&mut d, "thead", "", "Form and control¦Real score¦Control mean¦Control interval¦p¦Draws", "cols=0.31,0.11,0.12,0.22,0.11,0.10");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Plain, pooled gaps¦-0.157¦-0.213¦-0.236 to -0.190¦0.0005¦2,000");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Plain, order shuffled¦-0.157¦-0.153¦-0.161 to -0.145¦0.85¦2,000");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Blagg-Richardson style, order shuffled¦-0.141¦-0.125¦-0.134 to -0.116¦0.995¦200");
    attributed(&mut d, "row", "", "Gapped, order shuffled¦-0.079¦-0.078¦-0.081 to -0.074¦0.68¦200", "last=1");
    attributed(&mut d, "figure", "fig:reg", "Real regularity scores (diamonds) against the 5th to 95th percentile range (bars) and mean (ticks) of each control. Only the pooled-gap control is beaten. Drawn from the run's result file.", "src=fig-regularity.png;width=0.98;alt=Diamonds for the real scores against grey bars for the control ranges");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The Blagg-Richardson style form scores below its own order-shuffle mean, and the gapped form sits at its mean. The extra freedom in those forms fits shuffled systems as well as real ones.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "When an interior planet is dropped, the geometric mean of its two neighbours predicts its period better than the rule does: median error 0.107 against 0.131 in log period, a ratio of 1.23. Figure {ref:fig:loo} shows the two errors.");
    attributed(&mut d, "figure", "fig:loo", "Leave-one-out prediction of an interior planet's period: the plain form fitted to the other planets against the geometric mean of the two neighbours.", "src=fig-loo.png;width=0.98;alt=Two horizontal bars, the fit's error longer than the neighbours' error");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The Solar System was scored beside the test and is not part of it. It has {m:r} of 0.32 on the plain form and 0.17 on the gapped form, against a catalogue median of 0.157 on the plain form. On this statistic the median exoplanet system is more regular than ours.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Table {ref:tab:forecast} sets the forecasts written before the run against the outcomes.");
    labelled(&mut d, "tablecap", "tab:forecast", "Forecasts fixed in the preregistration against what the run gave.");
    attributed(&mut d, "thead", "", "Quantity¦Forecast¦Outcome", "cols=0.46,0.27,0.27");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Qualifying systems¦150¦122");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Median residual ratio, plain form¦0.22¦0.157");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Beats the pooled-gap control¦probability 0.8¦yes");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Beats the order-shuffle control, plain¦probability 0.15¦no");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Beats the order-shuffle control, gapped¦probability 0.15¦no");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Beats the order-shuffle control, Blagg style¦probability 0.2¦no");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Leave-one-out ratio¦1.15¦1.23");
    attributed(&mut d, "row", "", "Planted systems caught¦1.0¦yes", "last=1");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:discussion", "Discussion");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Hayes and Tremaine {cite:hayes1998} argued that a Titius-Bode law is what stable spacing looks like. Our result is a direct check of one consequence of that view on exoplanets. If the rule only describes similar gaps, then a system with its gaps shuffled should fit as well as the real one. It does, for three forms.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "The prediction test points the same way. A rule that captured a law of sequence should predict a missing planet better than a local average of its neighbours, because it uses the whole system. It predicts worse. Local spacing carries the information, which fits the correlated period ratios of neighbouring planets reported by Weiss and colleagues {cite:weiss2018}.");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "This bears on the prediction debate between Bovaird and Lineweaver {cite:bovaird2013} {cite:bovaird2015} and Huang and Bakos {cite:huang2014} in one way. A predicted planet in a gap is a claim that the gaps should be more equal than they look. That claim can be useful as a search target. It does not need a law of sequence, and it should be weighed against the neighbour baseline rather than against no prediction at all.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:limits", "Limitations");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The three forms were written from memory of the published ones, and a later review of our own ledger flagged that. A published form that differs could score differently, so the result covers these three versions only. Blagg's formula with its periodic term was not tested.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Transit surveys miss long periods and tilted planets, so the gaps are survey-shaped. The order shuffle keeps that effect, which is why it is the fair control, but the pooled-gap result is partly a survey result.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The catalogue's multi-planet entries are a selected set, and the sample is 122 systems, short of the 150 we forecast.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The 200-draw controls for the two richer forms give coarse p values.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "The finding says nothing about the physics that sets spacing.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Scoring was done by our own code and was not blind.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:verified", "Verified");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Verified: on 9 November 2026 the counts (122 systems, 543 planets), the three real scores, every control mean and percentile, the p values, the draw counts, the planted score, the two leave-one-out errors and their ratio, and the Solar System values were re-read from the run's result file. They match the findings ledger, our append-only, dated list of every recorded result, where corrections enter as new lines, old entries are never rewritten and failures stand beside successes. The preregistration's SHA-256 matches the hash recorded in the result file. Both figures were drawn by a script that reads only that result file. The study was not re-run for this paper. The literature in the introduction was found by searches run on 9 November 2026 and read at the level of abstracts and publisher pages; the full texts of the cited papers were not read.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:corrections", "Corrections");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "31 October 2026: a review of our ledger noted that the three forms were written from memory. This paper states it in the methods and the limitations.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "9 November 2026: first published version.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:repro", "Reproduction");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Data: query the NASA Exoplanet Archive Planetary Systems Composite Parameters table (pscomppars) for systems with four or more planets with orbital periods and a controversy flag of zero.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "For each system, sort planets by period and fit eqs. {ref:eq:plain}, {ref:eq:blagg} and {ref:eq:gapped}, the last with up to two inserted empty places chosen to minimise the residual.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Score each system with eq. {ref:eq:r}, take minus the median, and build both controls with the same fitting freedom: 2,000 draws for the plain form, 200 for the others.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Compute the leave-one-out ratio, eq. {ref:eq:loo}, over interior planets.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Preregistration SHA-256: c11616a0373890eb2c65e76375f2a8cd122223f4cd04a624a105d7f29f721b62. Plan SHA-256: 45f201bb0197a18dae140e101293b335e179b1bf0ecac6ae48a602712088fdb0. Run 20261031T220149Z.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "Cost: the whole run took under two minutes on one desktop CPU.");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:appendix", "Appendix A. The preregistered verdicts");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Each verdict is a set of rules written before the run. The word is ours for the pass or fail of one such rule set, read off by code, and it never covers a reading written after the numbers were seen. A verdict passes only when every rule holds. Values are read from the run's result file.");
    labelled(&mut d, "tablecap", "tab:verdicts", "Preregistered verdicts and the rule that decided each one.");
    attributed(&mut d, "thead", "", "Verdict¦Deciding rule¦Value¦Holds", "cols=0.26,0.48,0.13,0.13");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Instrument works¦planted score above pooled-gap 95th percentile (-0.190); at least 60 systems¦-0.050; 122¦yes");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Beats pooled gaps¦plain score above pooled-gap 95th percentile (-0.190)¦-0.157¦yes");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Beats order shuffle, plain¦plain score above order-shuffle 95th percentile (-0.145)¦-0.157¦no");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Beats order shuffle, Blagg style¦Blagg-style score above its order-shuffle 95th percentile (-0.116)¦-0.141¦no");
    blk(&mut d, "row", "Beats order shuffle, gapped¦gapped score above its order-shuffle 95th percentile (-0.074)¦-0.079¦no");
    attributed(&mut d, "row", "", "Law-like¦beats the plain order shuffle and leave-one-out ratio below 1¦-0.157; 1.23¦no", "last=1");
    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:glossary", "Glossary");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Each term is defined where it is first used in the text. The same definitions, with the field each comes from and what each excludes, are collected here.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:Titius-Bode rule}. Any formula that places a system's planets at distances or periods growing by a near-constant ratio from one planet to the next, here fitted in three forms to the log of orbital period. Field: planetary astronomy. Excludes a physical model of how planets form or migrate.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:peas in a pod}. The pattern, reported from Kepler data, that planets inside one system tend to be alike in size and in the ratios between neighbouring periods. Field: exoplanet surveys. Excludes a rule for the order of the gaps, and any likeness between planets of different systems.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:control}. A version of the data with the structure under test removed (for example shuffled or pooled), scored the same way as the real data, so the real score has something to beat. Field: statistics and experimental design. Excludes a comparison with a different dataset or with a published number.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:preregistration}. A dated written plan for a study, fixed and hash-locked before any result is seen: the question, the data, the statistic, the controls, the forecasts and the kill conditions. Field: research method (metascience). Excludes a plan revised after looking at the outcome, and a protocol written only for the record after the run.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:leave-one-out prediction}. Dropping one interior planet, fitting the rule to the others and comparing the predicted period with the true one, repeated for every interior planet. Field: statistics, cross-validation. Excludes scoring a planet with a fit that has already seen it.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:planted check}. Data built so that the right answer is known, run through the same analysis before the real data, to show that the analysis can see the effect when it is there. Field: research method; called a positive control in experimental science. Excludes any check made on the real data, which cannot show what the analysis misses.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:kill condition}. A rule written in the preregistration that, when met, decides against the claim with no room for judgement after the run. Field: research method (our usage, after Popper's falsification). Excludes reasons found after the run for setting a result aside.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:SHA-256}. A hash function that turns a file into a 64-character fingerprint which changes if any byte of the file changes; a recorded hash shows the file was not edited after it was taken. Field: cryptography. Excludes encryption, because the hash keeps nothing secret and cannot be turned back into the file.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:order-shuffle control}. Each system rebuilt from its own innermost period and its own gaps in log period, put in a random order, and scored with the same form and freedom as the real system. Field: our usage, a permutation control. Excludes gaps borrowed from other systems, which the pooled-gap control uses.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:pooled-gap control}. Each system rebuilt from gaps in log period drawn at random from all systems' gaps, and scored with the same form and freedom as the real system. Field: our usage, a resampling control. Excludes planets or gaps from outside the 122 systems in the sample.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:void}. The verdict of a run whose planted check failed or whose sample fell below its stated minimum; it says nothing for or against the claim. Field: research method (our usage). Excludes a null result, which comes from a working instrument.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:law-like}. The verdict that a Titius-Bode form beats the order-shuffle control's 95th percentile and predicts dropped planets better than the neighbour baseline. Field: our usage, fixed in the preregistration. Excludes regularity that similar gaps alone produce, which beats only the pooled-gap control.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:TAP service}. The Table Access Protocol of the International Virtual Observatory Alliance, the standard query interface through which astronomical archives return table rows. Field: astronomical data services. Excludes any selection or cleaning of the rows, which the query itself sets.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:controversy flag}. A catalogue field set to 1 when a planet's existence or confirmation has been questioned in the published literature and 0 otherwise. Field: NASA Exoplanet Archive catalogue fields. Excludes independent confirmation, which a flag of 0 does not give.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:transit survey}. A search that finds planets by the small dip in a star's light as a planet crosses its disc, which favours short periods and orbits seen close to edge-on. Field: observational astronomy. Excludes planets found only by other methods, such as radial velocity or direct imaging.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:root mean square residual}. The square root of the mean squared difference between fitted and observed log period across a system's planets, a measure of scatter about the fit. Field: statistics. Excludes any offset or trend the fit has already absorbed into its parameters.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:geometric mean}. For two numbers, the square root of their product, which is the plain average taken on a log scale; here the neighbour baseline for a dropped planet's period. Field: statistics. Excludes the ordinary average of the two periods, which is larger whenever they differ.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:p value (from draws)}. The share of control draws scoring at or above the real score, with one added to the count and to the number of draws, so it is never zero. Field: statistics. Excludes a p value from a textbook formula that assumes a distribution.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:findings ledger}. The append-only, dated list of every result we have recorded, numbered F1, F2 and on; corrections are added as new dated lines and old entries are never rewritten. Field: our records. Excludes a curated list of successes.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:verdict}. The pass or fail of one preregistered rule set against the run's numbers, read off by code. Field: research method (our usage). Excludes an interpretation written after reading the numbers.");
    blk(&mut d, "section", "References");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "nasa", "NASA Exoplanet Archive. Planetary Systems Composite Parameters table (pscomppars). NASA Exoplanet Science Institute, Caltech/IPAC. Retrieved 31 October 2026. https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "blagg1913", "Blagg, M. A. (1913). On a suggested substitute for Bode's law. Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society 73, 414-422. doi:10.1093/mnras/73.6.414");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "richardson1945", "Richardson, D. E. (1945). Distances of the planets from the Sun and of satellites from their primaries. Popular Astronomy 53, 14.");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "hayes1998", "Hayes, W. and Tremaine, S. (1998). Fitting selected random planetary systems to Titius-Bode laws. Icarus 135, 549-557. doi:10.1006/icar.1998.5999");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "bovaird2013", "Bovaird, T. and Lineweaver, C. H. (2013). Exoplanet predictions based on the generalized Titius-Bode relation. Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society 435, 1126-1138. doi:10.1093/mnras/stt1357");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "huang2014", "Huang, C. X. and Bakos, G. A. (2014). Testing the Titius-Bode law predictions for Kepler multi-planet systems. Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society 442, 674. arXiv:1405.2259");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "bovaird2015", "Bovaird, T., Lineweaver, C. H. and Jacobsen, S. K. (2015). Using the inclinations of Kepler systems to prioritize new Titius-Bode-based exoplanet predictions. Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society. arXiv:1412.6230");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "weiss2018", "Weiss, L. M., Marcy, G. W., Petigura, E. A. and others (2018). The California-Kepler Survey. V. Peas in a pod: planets in a Kepler multi-planet system are similar in size and regularly spaced. The Astronomical Journal 155, 48. doi:10.3847/1538-3881/aa9ff6");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "traxler1889", "Traxler, R. P. (1889). The Principles of Mechanics as Applied to the Solar System. San Francisco: C. A. Murdock and Co. Internet Archive item principlesofmech00traxiala.");
    let ok = save(&d, "F310.doc.tsv");
    println!("{} blocks ({})", d.blocks, ok);
}
